    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"gobbling up\" analogy was just a little creative license to help illustrate the point. Now, let's dive into the chemistry.\n\nThe balance between Lactobacillus and yeast in your sourdough starter is largely determined by the temperature. Lactobacillus, being a mesophilic organism, thrives in temperatures between 20°C and 30°C (68°F to 86°F). At these temperatures, Lactobacillus can grow rapidly and produce lactic acid, which lowers the pH of the starter.\n\nOn the other hand, yeast, particularly Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is a thermophilic organism that prefers warmer temperatures, typically between 25°C and 35°C (77°F to 95°F). At these temperatures, yeast can grow rapidly and produce CO2, which increases the pH of the starter.\n\nWhen temperature fluctuations occur, it can disrupt this delicate balance. If the temperature drops below 20°C (68°F), Lactobacillus growth slows down, and yeast can start to overgrow, leading to an increase in pH. Conversely, if the temperature rises above 30°C (86°F), yeast growth accelerates, and Lactobacillus can",
